Q: How do I properly use user inputs in conditional statements? A: <p>Instead of asking the user to enter true/false, it is common to ask the user yes/no, or simply y/n.</p>
<p>I would therefore recommend that you create a function <code>get_YN_from_user</code> which asks a question to the user and returns a <code>bool</code> value that is <code>true</code> if the user answered <code>&quot;yes&quot;</code> or <code>&quot;y&quot;</code>, or the corresponding upper-case letters, and <code>false</code> if the user answered <code>&quot;no&quot;</code> or <code>&quot;n&quot;</code>. If the user did not provide a meaningful answer, the function should automatically print an error message and reprompt the user for valid input. Here is an example program:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;cctype&gt;
#include &lt;stdexcept&gt;

bool get_YN_from_user( const std::string&amp; prompt )
{
    // repeat until input is valid
    for (;;)
    {
        std::string line;

        // prompt user for input
        std::cout &lt;&lt; prompt;

        // read one line of input from user
        if ( ! std::getline( std::cin, line ) )
        {
            throw std::runtime_error( &quot;Error reading input!\n&quot; );
        }

        // convert input line to lower-case
        for ( auto &amp;c : line )
        {
            c = std::tolower( static_cast&lt;unsigned char&gt;(c) );
        }

        // return true if user answered yes
        if ( line == &quot;y&quot; || line == &quot;yes&quot; )
        {
            return true;
        }

        // return false if user answered no
        if ( line == &quot;n&quot; || line == &quot;no&quot; )
        {
            return false;
        }

        // input is invalid, so print error message and restart loop
        std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Invalid input. Try again!\n&quot;;
    }
}

int main() try
{
    bool hungry = get_YN_from_user( &quot;Are you hungry (Y/N)? &quot; );
    bool  angry = get_YN_from_user( &quot;Are you  angry (Y/N)? &quot; );

    if ( hungry )
    {
        if ( angry )
        {
            std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;You're hangry!\n&quot;;
        }
        else
        {
            std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;You're hungry!\n&quot;;
        }
    }
    else
    {
        if ( angry )
        {
            std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;You're angry!\n&quot;;
        }
        else
        {
            std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;You're empty!\n&quot;;
        }
    }
}
catch ( const std::runtime_error&amp; e )
{
    std::cerr &lt;&lt; &quot;Runtime error: &quot; &lt;&lt; e.what() &lt;&lt; '\n';
}
</code></pre>
<p>This program has the following behavior:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>Are you hungry (Y/N)? y
Are you  angry (Y/N)? y
You're hangry!
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>Are you hungry (Y/N)? y
Are you  angry (Y/N)? n
You're hungry!
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>Are you hungry (Y/N)? n
Are you  angry (Y/N)? y
You're angry!
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>Are you hungry (Y/N)? n
Are you  angry (Y/N)? n
You're empty!
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>Are you hungry (Y/N)? q
Invalid input. Try again!
Are you hungry (Y/N)? yES
Are you  angry (Y/N)? abc
Invalid input. Try again!
Are you  angry (Y/N)? NO
You're hungry!
</code></pre>

Q: How to prevent any app to load my website in webview A: <blockquote>
<p>Being show inside a webview is as bad for security as being nested inside a cross domain iframe! it's absurd there's not a simple header that just causes Android/iOS to open the link in a proper browser instead...</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A <em>&quot;simple header&quot;</em> would not work because the <code>iframe</code> and the <code>webView</code> are sending the same type of signals as a <em>&quot;proper browser&quot;</em> would send anyway. A <strong>webView</strong> actually is a proper browser (engine-wise, like WebKit for example), an <strong>iframe</strong> exists inside a proper browser (when the HTML is rendered).  You will always detect <em>&quot;I am a proper browser&quot;</em> when these things request your data.</p>
<p>My understanding is that you want to stop random Android apps from being able to load your website content in an iframe or webView, eg:</p>
<pre><code>Android java: myWebView.loadUrl(&quot;https://www.example.com&quot;);

HTML5 markup: &lt;iframe src=&quot;https://www.example.com&quot;&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Maybe the attacker might even be <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/57551930/2057709">setting custom user-agents</a>.</p>
<p>You're not trying to handle advanced hackers who can create their own browsers in Assembly, correct?</p>
<p>See if the following advice below is enough to achieve your intended goal. <br>
The result should be something like this:</p>
<img src="https://i.sstatic.net/T5GNbGJj.png)">
<p><strong>(1) Detecting iframe:</strong></p>
<p><strong>(1)</strong> Using <code>window</code> property:</p>
<pre><code>//# detect iframe
if( (window.self !== window.top) || (window.location !== window.parent.location) )
{
    //# true == Your content is inside an iframe ...
}
else
{
    //# false == Is NOT in iframe ...
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>(2)</strong> Using Server-side language:</p>
<p>PHP scripts run in most servers so just serve to your users an <code>index.php</code> (instead of using an <code>index.html</code>) that can check origin of request HTTP_ORIGIN . This can be spoofed (faked) so you have to get creative and think of something unique to your website that a spoofer cannot give if requested).</p>
<p>Let me know if you want to go down the PHP route, or is just about detecting WebView...?</p>
<p><strong>(2) Detecting WebView:</strong></p>
<p>You can use client-side javascript to <strong>check the User Agent</strong>. This is secure because some details cannot be faked via the Android SDK due to recent <a href="https://android-developers.googleblog.com/2024/12/user-agent-reduction-on-android-webview.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">user-agent reduction</a> changes, and some limits of the Android API:<br>
Example: <br>See how you can read via <a href="https://developer.android.com/reference/androidx/webkit/UserAgentMetadata.BrandVersion" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>getBrand()</code></a> but cannot write with a not-existing <code>setBrand()</code>?.</p>
<p>This will have <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Navigator/userAgent" rel="nofollow noreferrer">useful (User-Agent detection) information</a> for you. Ask anything if stuck.</p>
<p>Some testable code for detecting if a <strong>WebView</strong> is used:<br>
PS: <a href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.snc.test.webview2&amp;hl=en_GB" rel="nofollow noreferrer">This app: WebView Test</a> will help you check how it looks on the WebView of your nearest Android device.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;body&gt;

&lt;div id=&quot;txt_ua-output&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div id=&quot;render_content&quot; style=&quot;position:absolute;&quot; &gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;script&gt;

let render_content = document.getElementById(&quot;render_content&quot;);
let ua_nav = window.navigator;
let ua_data = window.navigator.userAgent;
let ua_obj = {};

let isMobile = false; let isWebView = false; let typeOfWindow = -1;

//# try detect for Android and React WebView (add others if/when needed)
if( (window.Android) ) { typeOfWindow = &quot;Android SDK WebView&quot;; }
if( (window.ReactNativeWebView) ) { typeOfWindow = &quot;ReactNativeWebView&quot;; }

if( ua_data.includes(&quot;Mobile&quot;) || ua_data.includes(&quot;WebView&quot;) || ua_data.includes(&quot;wv&quot;) )
{
    if( ua_data.includes(&quot;WebView&quot;) || ua_data.includes(&quot;wv&quot;) ) { isWebView = true; }

    if( ua_data.includes(&quot;iOS/&quot;) || ua_data.includes(&quot;iPad&quot;) || ua_data.includes(&quot;iPod&quot;) ||
        ua_data.includes(&quot;Mobile&quot;) || ua_data.includes(&quot;Android&quot;) || ua_data.includes(&quot;iPhone&quot;) || 
        ua_data.includes(&quot;React&quot;) ||ua_data.includes(&quot;Samsung&quot;) || ua_data.includes(&quot;Tizen&quot;)
    )
    { if( ua_nav.maxTouchPoints &gt; 1 ) { isMobile = true; } }
}

//# then try to load main app content
load_app_content( isMobile );

function load_app_content( input_bool )
{
    if( input_bool == true )
    {
        //# load image or show warning: &quot;content is blocked&quot;
    }
    else
    { render_app_content( ua_nav ); }
}

function render_app_content( input )
{
    render_content.innerHTML = &quot;&lt;br&gt; You are using &lt;b&gt;&quot; + get_msg_about_browser( input ) + &quot; &quot;;
    render_content.innerHTML += &quot;&lt;br&gt; Is Mobile? &lt;b&gt;&quot; + isMobile + &quot;&lt;/b&gt; ... Is WebView? &lt;b&gt;&quot; + isWebView + &quot; &lt;/b&gt;&quot;;

    if( typeOfWindow != -1 ) // # has changed from the default setting of -1
    {
         render_content.innerHTML += &quot; ... DOM Window type: &lt;b&gt;&quot; + typeOfWindow + &quot; &lt;/b&gt;&quot;;
    }
}

function get_msg_about_browser( input )
{
    let str_browser = &quot;undefined&quot;; let str_os = &quot;undefined&quot;;

    //## estimate browser name
    if( input.vendor.includes(&quot;Google&quot;) ) { str_browser = &quot;Chrome&quot;; }
    if( input.vendor.includes(&quot;Apple Computer&quot;) ) { str_browser = &quot;Safari&quot;; }
    if( input.vendor.length == 0 ) { str_browser = &quot;Firefox&quot;; }

    //# estimate OS platform
    if( input.platform.includes(&quot;Android&quot;) ) { str_os = &quot;Android&quot;; }
    if( input.platform.includes(&quot;iOS&quot;) || input.platform.includes(&quot;Apple&quot;) ) { str_os = &quot;iOS&quot;; }

    if( input.platform.startsWith(&quot;Win&quot;) ) { str_os = &quot;Windows&quot;; }
    if( input.platform.includes(&quot;Mac&quot;) ) { str_os = &quot;Apple-Mac&quot;; }
    if( input.platform.includes(&quot;Linux&quot;) ) { str_os = &quot;Linux&quot;; }

    //# finish and go home... 
    return( str_browser + &quot; on &quot; + str_os + &quot; &quot; );
}

&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>
